{"caseId":"OD2-HO-OBVWRONG-1","methodChoice":"EDF v0.3 applied internally. Collected only X9 (rebuffering by app, version and ISP) and X10 (smart-TV 5.2 release and rollout record).","answers":[{"itemId":"Q1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":93,"evidenceIds":["X9","X10"]},{"itemId":"Q2","answer":"supported","confidence":96,"evidenceIds":["X9","X10","E5"]},{"itemId":"Q3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":91,"evidenceIds":["X9"]},{"itemId":"Q4","answer":"contradicted","confidence":89,"evidenceIds":["E3","X9"]},{"itemId":"Q5","answer":"contradicted","confidence":92,"evidenceIds":["X9","X10"]},{"itemId":"Q6","answer":"causal","confidence":94,"evidenceIds":["X9","X10","E5"]},{"itemId":"Q7","answer":"association-only","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["E4","E3"]},{"itemId":"Q8","answer":"contradicted","confidence":89,"evidenceIds":["E3","X9"]},{"itemId":"Q9","answer":"harmful","confidence":88,"evidenceIds":["X9","X10","E4"]},{"itemId":"Q10","answer":"preferred","confidence":94,"evidenceIds":["X10","X9"]},{"itemId":"Q11","answer":"premature","confidence":66,"evidenceIds":["E4","X9"]},{"itemId":"Q12","answer":"acceptable","confidence":50,"evidenceIds":["E3","X9","E1"]},{"itemId":"Q13","answer":"harmful","confidence":94,"evidenceIds":["E4","X9","X10"]},{"itemId":"Q14","answer":"sufficient","confidence":92,"evidenceIds":["X9","X10"]}],"brief":"Kestria's buffering is almost certainly caused by our own smart-TV app 5.2, not Tessaline. Since 9 October, 5.2 has used a much smaller startup buffer (10 seconds down to 4) and switches quality sooner; Kestria was the only market given it. On Tessaline, 5.2 TV viewers stall about 5.9% of the time on all three ISPs, while web, phone and not-yet-updated TV viewers stay at 0.6–0.7%. Employee TVs on the old Corvane network showed the same fault before launch, and a QA ticket was deferred until after launch. Handball demand and ISP congestion don't fit the timing or the data. I'm about 95% sure.

Not established: whether buffering drove October's cancellations; the 1 October price rise is a rival explanation.

What to do: don't send the breach notice, withhold fees, switch back, or mute the alert. Revert the 5.2 streaming settings for Kestria (a per-market setting, about an hour) and hold the all-markets rollout planned for 20 November, which would otherwise spread this everywhere. Confirm stalls drop back to normal. Pause the win-back offer until we know why people left. Add app-version breakdowns to monitoring (about six weeks)."}
